 The browser interface for Steve Howell's safari port.

 THE PAGE PAINTS DRAW COMMANDS, NOT PIXELS, and that is the port's own design
 rather than a choice made here. `port/Render.codex` and `port/Paint.codex`
 answer a `List DrawCmd` -- polygons, gradients and a beacon disc in screen
 coordinates -- and the original game's `blitter.js` paints exactly that onto a
 canvas. So this chapter is a SERIALISER: it writes the expanded command list
 into linear memory in the order the painter reads it, and the page paints it.
 Nothing here decides what is worth drawing; `port/Blit.codex` already did,
 which is why `blit-expand` runs before the write.

 THE WIRE IS f64 WHERE THE ORIGINAL IS f32, and that is the one place this
 deliberately differs from the upstream page. `Paint.codex` says its screen
 coordinates "narrow to f32 in the shim, at the buffer", which is true of the
 harness that built the upstream module. Narrowing costs a conversion this
 language has no builtin for, and buys nothing on a canvas that takes doubles,
 so the wire carries `real-to-bits` output straight through and our reader uses
 a DataView. A reader written for the upstream wire will NOT read this one.

 THE LAYOUT IS ONE RULE FOR EVERY TAG, which is the shape `Paint.codex` earned
 when it split `geom` from `pts`: tag, colour, then the second colour unless the
 tag has none, then the command's own parameters from `geom`, then the polygon
 as a point count and that many x,y pairs. A disc carries no polygon and stops
 after `geom`. Nothing reads a field its tag does not define.

   Each command is: tag, colour, the second colour unless the tag has none,
   STRENGTH, the geom LENGTH, that many geom values, then unless it is a disc
   the point count and that many x,y pairs.

 STRENGTH IS ON THE WIRE BECAUSE A DISC'S ALPHA LIVES THERE, and leaving it off
 was a real hole rather than a saving. The upstream painter reads a disc as
 colour then four floats, x y r alpha; `Paint.codex` puts x, y and r in `geom`
 and the alpha in the record's own `strength` field, so a wire carrying only
 `geom` hands the painter three numbers where it wants four. The decoder found
 it by reporting the disc's geom length as 3 against an assumed 4, which is the
 self-describing wire paying for itself the first time it was read.

   tag 0 SOLID         colour,                geom empty,   polygon
   tag 2 SPAN_SHADE    edge, middle,          x0 x1,        polygon
   tag 3 DISC          colour,                x y r alpha,  no polygon
   tag 4 RADIAL_POLY   centre, edge,          cx cy r,      polygon
   tag 5 LINEAR_POLY   c0, c1,                o0 o1 ax ay bx by, polygon
   tag 6 ELLIPSE_POLY  c0, c1,                o0 o1 cx cy ux uy vx vy, polygon

Section: The Buffer

 THE HEAP WALKS THROUGH THIS BUFFER EVERY FRAME, and that is the fact the whole
 chapter is arranged around. The buffer is at 12 MB and the state at 15 MB, and
 one frame's heap reaches 59,528,656 bytes (`sfw-heap-probe`), so a fixed
 address anywhere below that is inside the range the bump allocator crosses. The
 frame mark makes each frame start from the same low pointer; it does not keep
 anything safe from the frame that is running.

 So the invariant is an ORDER rather than a place: read anything held before
 anything allocates, and write anything that must survive after all allocation
 is done. Both halves were learned the hard way and both are recorded at the
 functions that keep them.

 `sfw-cap` is a refusal rather than a comment, because a serialiser that runs
 off the end of its buffer corrupts whatever is above it and still answers a
 plausible length.

  sfw-buf : Integer = #C00000

  sfw-cap : Integer = 3145728

Section: Writing Primitives

 A `Real` goes out as its IEEE-754 bits in two 32-bit halves, low first, which
 is what a little-endian DataView reads back as a float64. The split is
 `bit-and` and `bit-shr` rather than `int-mod` and division because the sign bit
 of a negative coordinate makes the arithmetic form answer a negative low word;
 `poke-32` writes four bytes, so the sign extension `bit-shr` leaves in the high
 half is discarded where it should be.

  sfw-w-u32 : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sfw-w-u32 (off) (v) =
    let d = poke-32 sfw-buf off v
    in off + 4

  sfw-w-real : Integer, Real -> Integer
  sfw-w-real (off) (v) =
    let b = real-to-bits v
    in let lo = poke-32 sfw-buf off (bit-and b 4294967295)
    in let hi = poke-32 sfw-buf (off + 4) (bit-and (bit-shr b 32) 4294967295)
    in off + 8

  sfw-w-reals : Integer, List Real, Integer -> Integer
  sfw-w-reals (off) (xs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length xs then off
    else sfw-w-reals (sfw-w-real off (list-at xs i)) xs (i + 1)

Section: Writing Commands

 THE GEOM LENGTH IS ON THE WIRE RATHER THAN IMPLIED BY THE TAG, and it is worth
 saying why because the first version did imply it. A reader that knows "tag 4
 carries three geom values" is encoding an assumption about `Paint.codex` that
 nothing checks, and when it is wrong the reader does not fail where the wrong
 field is: it desynchronises and reads a coordinate as a point count several
 commands later. Measured, that is exactly what happened -- absurd counts of
 1,232,924,824 at command 737 and 2,441,335,823 at command 144, with the first
 143 commands decoding perfectly. A self-describing wire costs four bytes a
 command and cannot drift from the chapter that fills it.

  sfw-w-cmd : Integer, DrawCmd -> Integer
  sfw-w-cmd (off) (c) =
    let o1 = sfw-w-u32 off c.tag
    in let o2 = sfw-w-u32 o1 c.color
    in let o3 = if c.tag == 0 then o2 else if c.tag == 3 then o2 else sfw-w-u32 o2 c.color2
    in let o4 = sfw-w-real o3 c.strength
    in let o5 = sfw-w-u32 o4 (list-length c.geom)
    in let o6 = sfw-w-reals o5 c.geom 0
    in if c.tag == 3 then o6
       else let o7 = sfw-w-u32 o6 (list-length c.pts / 2)
       in sfw-w-reals o7 c.pts 0

 The cap is checked between commands rather than inside one, so a refusal ends
 on a command boundary and the page paints a short frame instead of a corrupt
 one.

  sfw-w-cmds : Integer, List DrawCmd, Integer -> Integer
  sfw-w-cmds (off) (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then off
    else if off > sfw-cap then off
    else sfw-w-cmds (sfw-w-cmd off (list-at cs i)) cs (i + 1)

Section: The Held Ride

 THE RIDE IS HELD IN LINEAR MEMORY BETWEEN CALLS, and the first version's
 alternative is why. That one advanced from `ride-initial` on every call, so the
 cost of a frame grew with the step: measured on the built module, 13.9 ms at
 step 0, 15.7 at 100, 23.7 at 400 and 31.0 at 800, which is a screensaver that
 gets slower the longer anybody watches it. Holding the state makes a step O(1)
 and the frame cost flat.

 Reading a `Real` back needs `bits-to-real`, and it is worth saying that the
 wasm plug emits both bitcasts as the IDENTITY (`WasmEmitter.codex`): a `Real`
 there is already its f64 bits in an i64 slot, so there is nothing to convert.
 On x86-64 they are true bitcasts. Both roads mean the same thing.

  sfw-state : Integer = #F00000

  sfw-r-real : Integer -> Real
  sfw-r-real (off) =
    let lo = bit-and (peek-32 sfw-state off) 4294967295
    in let hi = bit-and (peek-32 sfw-state (off + 4)) 4294967295
    in bits-to-real (bit-or lo (bit-shl hi 32))

  sfw-s-real : Integer, Real -> Integer
  sfw-s-real (off) (v) =
    let b = real-to-bits v
    in let lo = poke-32 sfw-state off (bit-and b 4294967295)
    in let hi = poke-32 sfw-state (off + 4) (bit-and (bit-shr b 32) 4294967295)
    in off

  sfw-save : Ride -> Integer
  sfw-save (r) =
    let a = poke-32 sfw-state 8 r.rider.segment
    in let b = sfw-s-real 16 r.rider.along
    in let c = sfw-s-real 24 r.rider.across
    in let d = sfw-s-real 32 r.rider.yaw
    in let e = sfw-s-real 40 r.rider.v
    in let f = sfw-s-real 48 r.rider.tilt
    in let g = sfw-s-real 56 r.rider.heading
    in let h = sfw-s-real 64 r.rider.gaze-yaw
    in let i = sfw-s-real 72 r.rider.focus
    in let j = sfw-s-real 80 r.truck.pos
    in let k = sfw-s-real 88 r.truck.v
    in let l = poke-32 sfw-state 96 (if r.truck.braking then 1 else 0)
    in let m = sfw-s-real 104 r.clock
    in poke-32 sfw-state 0 1

  sfw-load : Integer -> Ride
  sfw-load (dummy) =
    Ride {
      rider = RiderState {
        segment = peek-32 sfw-state 8,
        along = sfw-r-real 16,
        across = sfw-r-real 24,
        yaw = sfw-r-real 32,
        v = sfw-r-real 40,
        tilt = sfw-r-real 48,
        heading = sfw-r-real 56,
        gaze-yaw = sfw-r-real 64,
        focus = sfw-r-real 72
      },
      truck = TruckState { pos = sfw-r-real 80, v = sfw-r-real 88, braking = peek-32 sfw-state 96 == 1 },
      clock = sfw-r-real 104
    }

  sfw-ride : Integer -> Ride
  sfw-ride (dummy) = if peek-32 sfw-state 0 == 1 then sfw-load 0 else ride-initial

  sfw-reset : Integer -> Integer
  sfw-reset (dummy) = poke-32 sfw-state 0 0

 THE STATE IS READ BEFORE ANYTHING ALLOCATES, for the same reason the header is
 written after everything does. `sfw-state` is at 15 MB and a frame's heap
 reaches 59 MB, so the saved ride is inside the range the bump allocator walks
 through. Reading it first means the read happens while it is still intact;
 writing it last means the write lands after the walking is done.

  sfw-forward : Integer -> Integer
  sfw-forward (dummy) =
    let h = __heap-save
    in let r0 = sfw-ride 0
    in let w = build-world
    in let r = ride-step w r0
    in let saved = sfw-save r
    in let restored = __heap-restore h
    in 0

Section: The Frame

 THE HEADER CARRIES WHAT THE PAGE PAINTS ITSELF. The backdrop is not in the
 command list and never was: the original page draws the sky band, the grass and
 the sun, and asks the guest only for the numbers, which is why a page that
 painted commands alone came out as geometry on a black field with no sky, no
 grass and no sun. The guest owns the two sky colours and the sun's placement;
 the grass colour and the gradient stops are the page's. The roll is here for the
 same reason: the whole frame banks into a turn, and the page rotates the canvas
 rather than the geometry.

   0  sky colour        u32
   4  horizon colour    u32
   8  sun visible       u32
  12  (pad)             u32
  16  sun x             f64
  24  sun y             f64
  32  sun scale         f64
  40  camera roll       f64
  48  the commands

 THE HEADER IS WRITTEN AS A LIST AND EVERY FIELD IS AN f64, and both halves of
 that are a bug fix rather than a taste.

 The first version wrote the fields one at a time through `sfw-w-u32` at
 constant offsets, and NOTHING LANDED. `sfw-w-u32` binds the poke to a `let` it
 does not use and answers `off + 4`; with every offset a constant the whole
 chain folds to 48 and the pokes, whose results nothing reads, go with it. The
 command path survives the same shape only because its offsets are
 data-dependent. The tell was two builds producing byte-identical buffers when
 the source between them had changed (L-SUSPECT), with `used` still exactly 48
 bytes longer than the headerless build: the arithmetic ran and the writes did
 not. `apps/c64/C64Wasm.codex` avoids this by SUMMING its poke results
 (`c64w-cpu-save` answers `d0 + d1 + ...`), which is what keeps them alive.

 Writing the header through `sfw-w-reals` puts it on the recursive path the
 commands already prove lands. Making every field an f64 is what lets it: the
 two colours go out through `real-from-int` and the page reads them back with a
 rounding conversion. Uniform width also means no alignment question.

   0  sky colour        f64 (an integer value)
   8  horizon colour    f64 (an integer value)
  16  sun visible       f64 (1.0 or 0.0)
  24  sun x             f64
  32  sun y             f64
  40  sun scale         f64
  48  camera roll       f64
  56  the commands

 THE COLOURS STAY INTEGERS AND ONLY RECORD-FIELD REALS CROSS AS f64, and that
 division is forced by a defect in our wasm plug rather than chosen.

 `real-to-bits` is emitted there as the IDENTITY, on the stated ground that a
 Real is already its f64 bits in an i64 slot. That holds for a Real read out of
 a record -- `geom`, `pts` and the held ride all round-trip correctly -- and it
 does NOT hold for one produced by `real-from-int` or written as a literal:
 those come back as values unrelated to the source, in a run where each is
 exactly four times the previous. Isolated by exporting `sky-color` as an
 Integer through the wrapper, which answers 9358054 on wasm and 9358054 on
 x86-64, so the arithmetic is sound on both roads and only the carrying is not.
 Steve's fork carries `e8486215 wasm: Real is an f64, not an i64 with f64 bits
 in it`; this is that, and it is the wasm lane's to fix rather than ours.

  sfw-w-header : Ride, SunPos -> Integer
  sfw-w-header (r) (s) =
    let a = sfw-w-u32 0 (sky-color r.clock)
    in let b = sfw-w-u32 a (horizon-color r.clock)
    in let c = sfw-w-u32 b (if s.visible then 1 else 0)
    in let d = sfw-w-u32 c 0
    in let e = sfw-w-real d s.x
    in let f = sfw-w-real e s.y
    in let g = sfw-w-real f s.scale
    in sfw-w-real g (rider-roll r.rider)

 EVERY ALLOCATION HAPPENS BEFORE THE FIRST BYTE IS WRITTEN, and the order here
 is the whole correctness of this function rather than a preference.

 The buffer sits at 12 MB inside a 16 MB linear memory and the bump heap grows
 up from below, so a frame that allocates its way past 12 MB writes THROUGH the
 buffer. The first version wrote the header, then called `ride-frame` and
 `blit-expand`, and those allocations overwrote it: the header came back as heap
 bytes (47467, which is 0xB96B, at offset 0) while the commands were perfect,
 because the commands are written last and nothing allocates after them. It
 reads exactly like a plug defect -- literals and computed values equally
 corrupt, the module's own `peek-32` agreeing with the page's DataView, and
 `sky-color` answering correctly when exported instead of written -- and it is
 not one. Build the whole frame first, then write.

  sfw-heap-probe : Integer -> Integer
  sfw-heap-probe (dummy) =
    let h = __heap-save
    in let w = build-world
    in let cmds = blit-expand (ride-frame w (sfw-ride 0)) 0
    in let top = __heap-save
    in let restored = __heap-restore h
    in top

  sfw-render : Integer -> Integer
  sfw-render (dummy) =
    let h = __heap-save
    in let r = sfw-ride 0
    in let w = build-world
    in let sun = ride-sun w r
    in let cmds = blit-expand (ride-frame w r) 0
    in let head = sfw-w-header r sun
    in let used = sfw-w-cmds head cmds 0
    in let kept = sfw-save r
    in let restored = __heap-restore h
    in used

 RENDERING RE-SAVES THE RIDE, and without it the screensaver restarts forever.
 The state lives at 15 MB and a frame's heap reaches 59 MB, so rendering walks
 through the saved ride exactly as it walks through the buffer. The page calls
 `sfw_render` and then `sfw_forward`, so by the time `forward` reads the state
 its flag has been overwritten, `sfw-ride` does not recognise it and answers
 `ride-initial`, and the ride begins again every single frame. Writing the ride
 back at the end of a render -- after all the allocation, like everything else
 that has to survive here -- leaves valid state behind whichever call ran last.

  sfw-buffer-at : Integer -> Integer
  sfw-buffer-at (dummy) = sfw-buf

  sfw-segments : Integer -> Integer
  sfw-segments (dummy) =
    let h = __heap-save
    in let n = list-length build-world
    in let restored = __heap-restore h
    in n

Section: Why There Is No Entry Here

 THIS CHAPTER DELIBERATELY HAS NO `opening`, and that is the whole difference
 between a browser module and a program. `apps/c64/C64Wasm.codex` has none
 either. Give this chapter an entry and the compiler treats it as a program and
 keeps only what the entry reaches: `sfw-buffer-at` returns a bare constant and
 had exactly one caller, so `inline-single-caller` folded it away, its
 definition vanished from the WAT, and the export wrapper had nothing to call.
 The build refused with "the module does not define: sfw_buffer_at", which is
 the wrapper check earning its keep. Without an entry every top-level
 definition is emitted and the wrappers bind.

 The serialiser was smoke-tested on the host before the entry came out, through
 a temporary opening: 19 segments, buffer at 12582912, and 342,604 bytes for
 frame 0 against 359,100 for frame 200 -- two different lengths, which is what
 says the ride actually advances rather than the same frame being written twice.